 "answer": "**No—not on the latest comparable hourly reading I found.** At **midnight on October 18, 2026** (local time), PM₂.₅ was **75.6 µg/m³ at Larkin in Johor Bahru**, versus **83 µg/m³ in northern Singapore** and **199 µg/m³ in western Singapore**. So Johor Bahru was slightly less hazy than the nearest Singapore region by that measure, and substantially less hazy than Singapore’s west. Both places still had poor air quality. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/stations/larkin-johor/))\n\nThat compares **hourly PM₂.₅**, rather than Malaysia’s API with Singapore’s PSI; those two indices should not be compared directly. Readings can change from hour to hour. ([nea.gov.sg](https://www.nea.gov.sg/our-services/pollution-control/air-pollution/faqs?utm_source=openai))",
